<$fill $name="body">

!! What it does

Drop a binary file (PDF, image, office document, archive, …) onto a tiddler in the story, or onto a stream row, and it becomes a tiddler right away: the file is stored in the wiki as base64, no `$:/Import` dialog, no server needed. A line that shows the attachment is appended to the end of the tiddler you dropped on: `<<derived-view …>>` (thumbnail, pages, text) when rimir/derived-view is installed, else a transclusion. It is ordinary text, so keep, move or delete it. Anything else is up to your wiki's configuration: link the new tiddler to the one you dropped on, give it fields.

Pasting works too: click a tiddler to select it (a thin bar on its left edge marks it), then paste a file or a screenshot.

!! How you use it

# Drag a file from your file manager onto a tiddler. The tiddler gets a dashed outline; release.
# The new tiddler is called `<tiddler>/<file name>` (configurable). A notice says what was attached.
# To paste: click the tiddler, then Ctrl+V. Without a selected tiddler a notice tells you to select one.
# Text files (`.md`, `.json`, `.tid`, `.csv`, …) are not attached: they go to TiddlyWiki's normal import, as before. Dropping outside any tiddler also behaves as before.

!! What the plugin does not decide

The plugin ships //no// linking. Linking to the drop target, extra fields, a kind type — all of that is a small action tiddler in your wiki (see //Examples//). The plugin provides the variables and runs your actions.

!! Getting the files out later

Base64 makes a single-file wiki big. A command-line run turns the attachments into external files plus `_canonical_uri` whenever you want (see //Reference → Extracting files//). On a Node.js server wiki TiddlyWiki already saves binary tiddlers as real files on disk.

